WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.415
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.415 --> 00:00:09.990
History is the foundation
of a people's identity.

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:15.150
And when that foundation is lost,
it is catastrophic.

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:20.900
This is ever true when it
comes to African history.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:24.610
This continent that made me,
the one that I love so

00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:28.230
much, has been scarred
by modern day labels.

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:34.880
We all know instability,
poverty, corruption.

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:39.150
But this is not who we are.

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:46.002
Three years ago, I was sitting in
my CEO's office in East Africa,

00:00:46.002 --> 00:00:50.265
when those words flashed across my mind,
I was working for

00:00:50.265 --> 00:00:55.050
a multinational company and
was being heavily encouraged to sign

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:59.403
off on financial statements
that were a complete fiction.

00:00:59.403 --> 00:01:01.950
I wanted this company to survive.

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:06.110
So I spent months meticulously
combing through these financials,

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:09.910
to make sure that the numbers were
accurate and actually useful.

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:15.290
Then one day,
my CEO calls me into a meeting.

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:17.260
She wasted no time.

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:19.290
She looked me right in the eyes and

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:23.200
she said, you are not doing
what I've asked you to do.

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:30.795
If you do not sign off,
I will destroy your reputation in Africa.

00:01:30.795 --> 00:01:34.222
I could not breathe.

00:01:34.222 --> 00:01:36.610
It felt like my body stopped working.

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:41.710
My mouth was so dry,
I was wondering if there was sand in it.

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:48.180
In this moment, I was about to
lose my job and my reputation and

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:52.930
all I could think was,
this is not who we are.

00:01:54.450 --> 00:02:00.130
Here I was about to be another
casualty of the modern day narrative.

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:02.490
This cannot be who we are.

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:07.300
And it's not because our history
cries out a different reality.

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:14.620
In one illustration, we have the Edo
people from 14th century West Africa,

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:16.630
a people of which I am a descendant.

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:22.307
This city operated with
such a well-organized and

00:02:22.307 --> 00:02:27.956
sophisticated communication channel
that if the city was under threat,

00:02:27.956 --> 00:02:33.441
20,000 warriors can be notified and
ready for battle in one day.

00:02:33.441 --> 00:02:35.473
Innovation was everywhere,

00:02:35.473 --> 00:02:40.440
their whole city was fortified with
the largest earthwork in the world.

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:44.320
They trusted their King
to protect his people.

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:48.770
Yet no decision was made without
the approval of the Queen Mother.

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:52.870
And women weren't only sought out for
their wisdom.

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:57.680
They also protected their city by forming
their own armies and engaging in battle.

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:02.780
The natural resources of brass, copper and

00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:07.510
ivory were used to create economic
stability through trade, and

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:11.170
also create beautiful striking artwork.

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:18.050
These people were not perfect, but
they were dedicated to their values.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:25.370
In the 15th century, this vibrant
kingdom would be changed forever.

00:03:25.370 --> 00:03:28.540
They would experience colonizers,

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:32.710
competence in conquering without
contemplating consequence.

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:39.260
After years of fighting for
survival, first with diplomacy and

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:42.860
then on the battlefield,
their city was burned to the ground.

00:03:44.660 --> 00:03:49.470
Beyond the lives that were lost,
beyond the arts that was stolen,

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:52.972
these people were forced to
exist in a different way.

00:03:52.972 --> 00:03:59.520
New culture, new religion,
new principles, carelessly scrambled

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:05.410
into a complex system that had been
carefully sculpted over hundreds of years.

00:04:05.410 --> 00:04:07.260
It worked like a cancer.

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:13.330
Defined plans for
succession were uprooted and distrust was

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:18.280
planted among leaders leading to
political instability, medicinal and

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:24.850
scientific innovation was discouraged and
devolved into daily hacks for survival.

00:04:25.870 --> 00:04:29.120
Generosity and
giving based on the seasons and

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:34.655
the times turned into transactional
exchanges motivated by self-interest.

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:42.180
This sad part of this story is
that eerily similar versions of

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:46.690
that story can be told about
dynasties across Africa.

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:50.309
Be at the kingdom of
Mutapa in Southern Africa,

00:04:50.309 --> 00:04:55.283
the Swahili to the East,
the Kingdom of Ghana in West Africa and so

00:04:55.283 --> 00:05:00.541
many other dynasties that would take
me the rest of this talk to list.

00:05:00.541 --> 00:05:07.340
The African identity today has been
melted into one messy narrative struggle.

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:10.610
And we hear about it all the time.

00:05:10.610 --> 00:05:14.525
Inflation in Zimbabwe, which is 175%.

00:05:14.525 --> 00:05:18.090
30,000 people in Nigeria
have been displaced

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:20.120
due to the activity of Boko Haram.

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:25.640
Armed militants killed over
40,000 civilians in Mali, and

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:27.524
that was just in 2019.

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:34.270
Now let's be clear, we should not forget
the beauty, the cultural phenomena,

00:05:34.270 --> 00:05:38.460
and the packets of progress that
exist on the continent today.

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:43.690
Powerful and passionate people,
African and not have put their money,

00:05:43.690 --> 00:05:49.020
their research, their technology, and even
their lives to improve that continent.

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:52.650
But if we continue to ignore our past,

00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:56.090
we will perpetually
misunderstand our presence and

00:05:56.090 --> 00:06:00.840
create well meaning but
an adequate solutions for our future.

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:07.310
Now, I believe in the power of knowing and

00:06:07.310 --> 00:06:12.740
internalizing history, because I have
witnessed it transform democracies and

00:06:12.740 --> 00:06:17.436
the evidence is in this country,
even right here in this room.

00:06:17.436 --> 00:06:20.190
And that's black America.

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:23.640
Now what he gave black America
remember its Holidays or

00:06:23.640 --> 00:06:28.040
space in American history books for
the heroes of the past?

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:34.140
Black America history matters today
because people fought for it to matter.

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:39.090
Left it to some state governments, slavery
would be called the Atlantic triangular

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:45.160
trade, and abolitionists would be written
in as radicals or dangerous or not at all.

00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:52.660
So when systems ignore the past by
slapping new names on old tricks, and

00:06:52.660 --> 00:06:58.340
like things about mass incarceration and
what police brutality are today, voices

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:04.040
rise up armed themselves with the truths
of history and fight to course correct.

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:04.660
We, too,

00:07:04.660 --> 00:07:09.530
in Africa can command the truths of our
history on our path towards progress.

00:07:10.620 --> 00:07:15.020
So with that, I have one ask
of everybody in this room and

00:07:15.020 --> 00:07:16.720
anybody watching this video.

00:07:17.730 --> 00:07:21.790
I asked that you study one
story from African history.

00:07:21.790 --> 00:07:23.920
There's so many good ones,

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:28.590
like the story of Lukeni Lua Nimi,
it's okay if you didn't catch that.

00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:31.635
Just Google the first king of the Congo.

00:07:31.635 --> 00:07:35.470
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; He was so thoughtful in his territorial

00:07:35.470 --> 00:07:40.420
expansion, that he gave all of his new
districts the rights to protest and

00:07:40.420 --> 00:07:42.450
reform his decisions.

00:07:42.450 --> 00:07:47.390
That led to peace throughout his reign,
and for many years after.

00:07:47.390 --> 00:07:50.880
Or what about the story of
King Caleb of the Kingdom of Aksum,

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:52.990
which is modern day Eritrea?

00:07:52.990 --> 00:07:55.950
He would send his armies across the ocean

00:07:55.950 --> 00:08:00.080
to protect people who are going
through religious persecution.

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:04.950
For those of you in this room who are not
from Africa, familiarizing yourself

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:10.570
with these stories, brings insights,
builds empathy and creates kinship.

00:08:10.570 --> 00:08:15.590
Like my bro Anna talked about over here,
it's like learning a new language.

00:08:15.590 --> 00:08:18.390
At first, you have little understanding.

00:08:18.390 --> 00:08:20.990
Not because the language
is lacking in any way, but

00:08:20.990 --> 00:08:23.850
because of your lack of knowledge of it.

00:08:23.850 --> 00:08:29.430
As you continue to learn more,
you open up yourself to a whole new world.

00:08:29.430 --> 00:08:31.830
Abstract, transcendence, and meaning and

00:08:31.830 --> 00:08:36.460
you build a genuine
connection to my Africans.

00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:41.750
It is time that we use our history as
a guiding light for the way forward,

00:08:41.750 --> 00:08:47.740
there is an uncompromising power in
reclaiming back those identities.

00:08:47.740 --> 00:08:52.070
It transforms the way that we see
ourselves and the way we see each other.

00:08:52.070 --> 00:08:57.210
And it informs the decisions we
make no matter how tough they are.

00:08:57.210 --> 00:09:01.100
Decisions like the one I had to
make in that office in East Africa.

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:05.370
That day, I chose to stand my ground.

00:09:05.370 --> 00:09:07.800
Even though I was visibly shaken,

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:12.840
my foundation was not because
that label is not who I am.

00:09:12.840 --> 00:09:17.780
I am the descendent of intentional
leaders who value justice.

00:09:17.780 --> 00:09:22.590
I am the daughter of great women
who fought mighty your battles.

00:09:22.590 --> 00:09:25.320
That is the Africa that
I choose to believe in.

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:27.850
That is the Africa that I carry with me.

00:09:27.850 --> 00:09:29.616
That is who we are.

00:09:29.616 --> 00:09:31.796
Thank you.

00:09:31.796 --> 00:09:32.768
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:32.768 --> 00:09:37.502
[MUSIC]

